    New -> Rollout [color="#D98E2B", penwidth=1.3, style=dotted,
                    label="  3. Simulate  ", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
}
```

- @Remark@: backpropagation (phase 4) increments $N$ at every red node on the
  path, root, B, and B2, and updates $Q$ the same way
  - Phase 3 (the rollout) never touches the stored tree, only the path does

* Phase 1: Selection

- @Goal@: walk from the root down to a node that is not yet fully expanded,
  deciding at each step which child to descend into

- @Problem@: the choice trades off two conflicting goals
  - **Exploit**: descend into the child with the best current mean $Q(s, a)$
  - **Explore**: descend into a child whose mean is still poorly estimated

- @Naive Solution@: always take node with the best mean
  - Cons: a child that was unlucky in its first playouts is never revisited,
    so the search locks onto the wrong line

- @Solution@: treat the node as a bandit and descend into the child with the
  highest optimistic index

* UCT: Upper Confidence Bounds for Trees

- @Definition@: **UCT** applies an optimistic bandit index at every node of
  the tree (Kocsis and Szepesvari, 2006)
  $$
  a^*
    = \argmax_{a \in A(s)}
        \left[
          \underbrace{Q(s, a)}_{\text{exploitation}}
          + c \underbrace{\sqrt{\frac{\ln N(s)}{N(s, a)}}}_{\text{exploration}}
        \right]
  $$
  where
  - $Q(s, a) \in [0, 1]$ is the mean outcome of playouts through edge
    $(s, a)$
  - $N(s)$ is the number of visits to $s$
  - $N(s, a)$ is the number of visits to the edge $(s, a)$
  - $c > 0$ is the exploration constant

- @Remark@: a child with $N(s, a) = 0$ has an infinite index, so every action
  is tried once before any is repeated

- @Intuition@: the bonus shrinks as an edge is sampled and grows for edges the
  search has been ignoring

* The Exploration Constant

- @Problem@: $c$ controls how wide the search spreads, and no single value
  works everywhere

- @Properties@
  - $c \to 0$: greedy descent, deep and narrow tree, may miss the best action
  - $c$ large: near-uniform descent, the tree stays shallow
  - Theory suggests $c = \sqrt{2}$ for rewards in $[0, 1]$

- @Limitations@: the theoretical value assumes stationary rewards, which does
  not hold inside a tree
  - Tuned values are domain-specific and often well below $\sqrt{2}$

- @Techniques@: reduce the sensitivity to $c$
  - Normalize $Q$ to the value range observed at each node
  - Replace the plain bonus with a learned prior over actions

* Phase 2: Expansion

- @Goal@: grow the tree by one node, so memory is spent only where the search
  actually goes

- @Variants@
  - _One node per iteration_: the standard choice, cheapest and most common
  - _Full expansion_: add all children of the leaf at once, viable only for a
    small branching factor
  - _Progressive widening_: reveal the $k$-th child only once
    $N(s) \ge \alpha k^{\beta}$, needed when $b$ is huge or continuous

- @Remark@: expansion is skipped when the leaf is terminal
  - The exact terminal value is backed up instead of a simulated one

- @Key idea@: the tree stores what is worth remembering, and the rollout
  throws the rest away

* Phase 3: Simulation

- @Goal@: obtain one cheap sample of the outcome that follows the new node

- @Definition@: the **default policy**, also called the **rollout policy**,
  plays from the new node to a terminal state and returns the terminal
  outcome $z$
  - Nothing visited during the rollout is added to the tree

- @Properties@: what makes a good rollout
  - _Fast_: the rollout cost multiplies the whole budget
  - _Unbiased enough_: systematic errors do not average away
  - _Diverse_: a deterministic rollout collapses the estimate onto one
    trajectory

- @Remark@: rollout quality and rollout speed trade off directly
  - A stronger policy gives better samples, but fewer of them

* Rollout Policies

- @Variants@
  - _Uniform random_: no knowledge at all, surprisingly effective in Go
  - _Heuristic_: hand-written patterns, capture rules, move filters
  - _Learned_: a small policy network trained to imitate strong play
  - _Truncated_: stop after $k$ steps and score the state with a learned value
    function

- @Counterexample@: a stronger rollout policy can make the search _weaker_
  - A deterministic strong policy makes every rollout from a node identical,
    so extra playouts carry no new information

- @Key idea@: rollouts exist to estimate a value, not to play well

* Phase 4: Backpropagation

- @Goal@: push the outcome $z$ back along the path from the new node to the
  root

- @Algorithm@: for every edge $(s, a)$ on that path
  $$
  N(s, a) \leftarrow N(s, a) + 1,
  \quad
  Q(s, a) \leftarrow Q(s, a) + \frac{z - Q(s, a)}{N(s, a)}
  $$
  - This is the incremental form of the running mean

- @Properties@
  - Only the visited path is touched, so an update costs $O(d)$ with $d$ the
    path length
  - Statistics never expire, so early noisy playouts keep some weight forever

- @Variants@: **max backup** stores the best child value instead of the mean
  - Sharper near the end of a search, but the variance early on is much higher

* Backpropagation in Two-Player Games

- @Problem@: a single outcome $z$ means opposite things to the two players

- @Solution@: store values from the point of view of the player to move, and
  flip the sign at each level
  $$z_{parent} = - z_{child}$$
  - Equivalent to minimax run on averaged, rather than exact, values

- @Example@: with $z \in \{-1, 0, +1\}$ for loss, draw, and win
  - A win for Black backs up as $+1$ at Black's nodes and $-1$ at White's

- @Remark@: single-agent planning uses no sign flip, and $z$ is the cumulative
  reward of the trajectory

* UCT: Worked Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Example@: at the root $N(s) = 10$, $\ln N(s) = 2.30$, and $c = 1.4$

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ([Child], [$N(s,a)$], [$Q(s,a)$], [Bonus], [UCT index]),
  rows: (
    ([A], [5], [0.60], [0.95], [1.55]),
    ([B], [3], [0.67], [1.23], [1.90]),
    ([C], [2], [0.50], [1.50], [$bold(2.00)$]),
  ),
  caption: "One selection step at the root",
  col-widths: (1fr, 1fr, 1fr, 1fr, 1fr),
  bold-first-col: true
)
```

- @Interpretation@: C is selected although it has the _worst_ mean
  - Its bonus $1.4 \sqrt{2.30 / 2} = 1.50$ outweighs the $0.17$ deficit in
    mean value

- @Remark@: after a few more visits to C the bonus shrinks and B takes over,
  unless C's playouts justify the attention
